WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.166 --> 00:00:07.070
Narrator: A Star Is Born

00:00:07.070 --> 00:00:09.630
has been nothing short of a phenomenon.

00:00:09.630 --> 00:00:12.569
It's grossed over 400
million dollars worldwide,

00:00:12.569 --> 00:00:16.840
far surpassing its reported
36 million dollar budget.

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:20.749
The soundtrack debuted at
number one on the Billboard 200

00:00:20.749 --> 00:00:24.250
and the movie was nominated
for eight Academy Awards

00:00:24.250 --> 00:00:26.429
including Best Picture.

00:00:26.429 --> 00:00:28.340
Before breaking our hearts

00:00:28.340 --> 00:00:29.980
the movie makes audiences believe

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:32.317
in the power of love and music.

00:00:32.317 --> 00:00:34.820
One way it did that early in the movie

00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:38.840
was through those huge
concert scenes that felt real.

00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:41.490
Well, it turns out they
were pretty much real.

00:00:41.490 --> 00:00:43.150
Bradley Cooper and Lady Gaga

00:00:43.150 --> 00:00:46.048
were singing live in those scenes.

00:00:57.437 --> 00:01:02.117
♪ I'm off the deep end,
watch as I dive in. ♪

00:01:02.117 --> 00:01:05.700
♪ I'll never meet the ground ♪

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:09.390
But that's not all that
made these scenes great.

00:01:09.390 --> 00:01:11.800
The film's sound team worked tirelessly

00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:13.310
during and after filming

00:01:13.310 --> 00:01:15.340
to make sure each sound would coalesce

00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:18.550
into the experience millions
of us had in theaters.

00:01:18.550 --> 00:01:20.240
We got a behind the scenes look

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:22.080
at how they mixed and layered sounds

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:23.920
for one of the toughest projects

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:26.233
sound wise, in recent history.

00:01:27.210 --> 00:01:30.310
This is the movie's team of
sound editors and mixers.

00:01:30.310 --> 00:01:32.975
Dean Zupancic, Steve Morrow

00:01:32.975 --> 00:01:35.491
Tom Ozanich and Alan Murray

00:01:36.370 --> 00:01:39.380
The team was nominated for
the Best Sound Mixing Oscar

00:01:39.380 --> 00:01:41.813
and the films most famous song, Shallow

00:01:41.813 --> 00:01:44.260
was nominated for Best Song.

00:01:44.260 --> 00:01:45.960
To give us the best possible idea

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:48.350
of how sound impacted A Star is Born

00:01:48.350 --> 00:01:49.750
the team offered to break down

00:01:49.750 --> 00:01:52.880
the climatic scene that features Shallow.

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:55.310
If you've seen the movie,
you know the scene.

00:01:55.310 --> 00:01:58.060
Ally: Maybe that could work,
like as a chorus or something.

00:02:00.066 --> 00:02:05.066
♪ I'm off the deep end. ♪

00:02:05.073 --> 00:02:06.640
♪ Watch as I dive in. ♪

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:09.060
Narrator: After teaching
Jack, Bradley Cooper

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:11.460
her song Shallow in a parking lot

00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:13.264
Ally, that's Lady Gaga

00:02:13.264 --> 00:02:15.770
stands backstage at Jack's concert

00:02:15.770 --> 00:02:17.533
as he begins singing her song.

00:02:17.533 --> 00:02:20.634
♪ Tell me something girl. ♪

00:02:20.634 --> 00:02:23.120
He invites her to join him on stage

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:24.965
and as the title of the movie suggests

00:02:24.965 --> 00:02:28.434
she's immediately launched
into super stardom.

00:02:28.434 --> 00:02:31.851
♪ Tell me something boy. ♪

00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:36.560
The whole sequence of events

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:38.290
starting with Ally's decision to quit

00:02:38.290 --> 00:02:40.513
her waitress job and head to the show

00:02:40.513 --> 00:02:42.143
to the end of Shallow

00:02:42.143 --> 00:02:44.873
lasts about 9 minutes and 30 seconds.

00:02:49.030 --> 00:02:52.320
There are five essential sound
elements in this sequence.

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:56.310
The process of layering helps
squeeze in every little sound.

00:02:56.310 --> 00:02:58.880
If you listen to each
layer of sound isolated

00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:00.493
it's a little disorienting.

00:03:12.350 --> 00:03:14.310
Jack: I'm going to sing
it either way so...

00:03:16.100 --> 00:03:20.500
♪ Are you happy in this modern world? ♪

00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:24.520
Dean: There's the sound behind the stage.

00:03:24.520 --> 00:03:26.500
There's the sound on the stage.

00:03:26.500 --> 00:03:28.640
But there's also the
sound into the audience

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:32.940
and we had to deliver on
those three difference planes.

00:03:32.940 --> 00:03:34.753
Narrator: So let's take them one by one.

00:03:37.630 --> 00:03:39.200
Without music playing

00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:41.870
you'll hear some effects
that are typically invisible

00:03:41.870 --> 00:03:43.870
but become much more pronounced

00:03:43.870 --> 00:03:46.250
like the sound of a car door opening

00:03:46.250 --> 00:03:47.994
and the sound of footsteps.

00:03:49.780 --> 00:03:52.570
These are known as Foley Sounds.

00:03:52.570 --> 00:03:55.730
They are based on an actor's
interactions and movements.

00:03:55.730 --> 00:03:59.050
The sounds of footsteps are
usually made by a Foley artist

00:03:59.050 --> 00:04:01.487
stomping around on a sound stage.

00:04:01.487 --> 00:04:03.679
Gail: He's so excited that you're here.

00:04:04.287 --> 00:04:05.833
Gail: Hi Ally, I'm Gail.

00:04:06.670 --> 00:04:07.503
Ally: Hi.

00:04:07.503 --> 00:04:08.420
Gail: Hi, oh you guys can leave your bags.

00:04:08.420 --> 00:04:09.640
We'll grab them in a sec.

00:04:09.640 --> 00:04:10.473
Ready?

00:04:10.473 --> 00:04:11.306
Ally: Okay, sure.

00:04:11.306 --> 00:04:12.300
Alan: The most important job, again

00:04:12.300 --> 00:04:14.120
is to get the dialogue clear enough

00:04:14.120 --> 00:04:16.090
so that they audience understands

00:04:16.090 --> 00:04:17.520
every word the actor's saying

00:04:17.520 --> 00:04:20.410
cause you've got to follow the story

00:04:20.410 --> 00:04:21.243
to get involved with the movie.

00:04:21.243 --> 00:04:23.353
Jack: I did an arrangement,
it was kind of not so great

00:04:23.353 --> 00:04:24.220
but uh maybe...

00:04:24.220 --> 00:04:26.097
Narrator: Even with how
loud the whole scene is

00:04:26.097 --> 00:04:28.750
one of the movie's most
memorable exchanges

00:04:28.750 --> 00:04:30.333
can still be heard.

00:04:39.722 --> 00:04:40.985
Gail: Hi Ally, I'm Gail.

00:04:42.620 --> 00:04:43.620
Hi, oh you guys can leave your bags.

00:04:43.620 --> 00:04:44.770
We'll grab them in a sec.

00:04:44.770 --> 00:04:45.603
Ready?

00:04:45.603 --> 00:04:46.436
Ally: Okay, sure.

00:04:52.250 --> 00:04:54.330
Narrator: You can hear the
band as background music

00:04:54.330 --> 00:04:56.930
even before Ally has reached the stage.

00:04:56.930 --> 00:04:58.166
It provides a perfect

00:04:58.166 --> 00:05:00.683
heart-thumping soundtrack to the scene.

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:07.520
While the singing was live

00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:10.320
the instruments we hear
were actually playback.

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:12.649
The band would mime along to the sound.

00:05:12.649 --> 00:05:15.650
While they let the band
play live in some takes

00:05:15.650 --> 00:05:17.850
all of what you hear in the final movie

00:05:17.850 --> 00:05:20.273
is pre-recorded instrumental playback.

00:05:35.504 --> 00:05:36.337
Jack: How are you? You made it.

00:05:36.337 --> 00:05:37.170
Ally: I'm good. Hi.

00:05:37.170 --> 00:05:38.377
Jack: It's so good to see you.

00:05:39.730 --> 00:05:41.900
Narrator: The sounds that
the concert crowd make

00:05:41.900 --> 00:05:44.327
are a mix of pre-recorded digital sound

00:05:44.327 --> 00:05:47.063
and sounds actually captured on set.

00:05:49.150 --> 00:05:52.840
During filming, the sound
team put a surround sound mic

00:05:52.840 --> 00:05:56.420
in the crowd as well as mics
on each side of the stage.

00:05:56.420 --> 00:05:59.653
This helped them capture the
appropriate ambient sound.

00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:02.790
Steve: I mean, we never stopped
them when we were shooting.

00:06:02.790 --> 00:06:03.623
We were just like, "Go for it.

00:06:03.623 --> 00:06:05.193
If you are excited, be excited."

00:06:06.360 --> 00:06:08.370
Narrator: You can hear
the live crowd react

00:06:08.370 --> 00:06:09.570
during this one snippet.

00:06:18.861 --> 00:06:20.150
The additional crowd sounds

00:06:20.150 --> 00:06:22.923
were carefully selected
from a library of sounds

00:06:22.923 --> 00:06:25.750
and that's not as easy as it seems.

00:06:25.750 --> 00:06:28.690
You can't just use any
old cheering noises.

00:06:28.690 --> 00:06:31.540
For instance, people
cheering at a football game

00:06:33.300 --> 00:06:36.050
are going to sound different
than the crowd at a concert.

00:06:38.461 --> 00:06:40.190
Jack: Thank you very much.

00:06:40.190 --> 00:06:42.912
♪ I'll never meet the ground ♪

00:06:42.912 --> 00:06:45.872
♪ Crash through the surface ♪

00:06:45.872 --> 00:06:48.203
♪ where they can't hurt us ♪

00:06:48.203 --> 00:06:53.203
♪ We're far from the shallow now ♪

00:06:53.741 --> 00:06:56.820
♪ In the shallow, shallow ♪

00:06:56.820 --> 00:06:59.590
Narrator: Of course, perhaps
nothing was more important

00:06:59.590 --> 00:07:01.523
than actually hearing the actors sing.

00:07:02.480 --> 00:07:05.060
Steve: Most musicals are
playback and lip-sync

00:07:05.060 --> 00:07:06.360
and those all have their challenges.

00:07:06.360 --> 00:07:09.710
This one, on the production side, was

00:07:10.630 --> 00:07:11.780
more challenging in the sense

00:07:11.780 --> 00:07:13.320
that it was all done live

00:07:13.320 --> 00:07:15.240
and you couldn't miss any take.

00:07:15.240 --> 00:07:17.490
Every take that they gave

00:07:17.490 --> 00:07:20.140
they put their heart and
soul in every single moment.

00:07:25.010 --> 00:07:27.000
Narrator: To get the crowds really going

00:07:27.000 --> 00:07:28.650
and allow viewers in movie theaters

00:07:28.650 --> 00:07:31.020
to feel like they were part of that crowd

00:07:31.020 --> 00:07:34.460
they of course had to make sure
the vocals sounded perfect.

00:07:34.460 --> 00:07:35.873
They created custom reverb

00:07:35.873 --> 00:07:39.320
an effect that adds
depth and echo to sound.

00:07:39.320 --> 00:07:40.500
Steve: We mapped the space

00:07:40.500 --> 00:07:42.985
to give the audience the feeling that

00:07:42.985 --> 00:07:45.960
all the vocals were pumped
live through the venue

00:07:45.960 --> 00:07:48.940
and you would hear it as you
would hear it in the venue.

00:07:48.940 --> 00:07:49.840
Narrator: And it ensures

00:07:49.840 --> 00:07:51.860
that any sounds recorded in the studio

00:07:51.860 --> 00:07:54.396
sound exactly like they
were recorded on set.

00:07:54.396 --> 00:07:56.870
It's a big reason why it feels like

00:07:56.870 --> 00:07:58.593
you're actually at this show.

00:08:01.700 --> 00:08:04.665
In the end, they stitched
every little sound together

00:08:04.665 --> 00:08:07.140
for an experience that constantly shifts

00:08:07.140 --> 00:08:09.654
between multiple dizzying perspectives.

00:08:09.654 --> 00:08:12.326
The screaming, the guitar rifts

00:08:12.326 --> 00:08:15.174
the singing, the Foley footsteps

00:08:15.174 --> 00:08:19.130
make for an overwhelming,
even awe-inspiring whole

00:08:19.130 --> 00:08:20.934
and that was the whole point.

00:08:20.934 --> 00:08:23.810
A Star is Born broke a lot of rules

00:08:23.810 --> 00:08:26.800
that other movies with
musical performances follow

00:08:26.800 --> 00:08:30.460
and that's part of the reason
it connected with audiences.

00:08:30.460 --> 00:08:32.200
Dean: [Director Bradley Cooper]
gave us the freedom to be...

00:08:34.380 --> 00:08:37.116
Steve: Creative.
Dean: Creative and vulnerable

00:08:37.116 --> 00:08:38.790
as well, you know, because...

00:08:39.960 --> 00:08:42.900
The mixing stage, just like being on set

00:08:42.900 --> 00:08:46.814
there might not be as much
of a focus of people on us

00:08:46.814 --> 00:08:49.113
but you're on a stage

00:08:49.113 --> 00:08:51.603
and you're scrutinized

00:08:51.603 --> 00:08:54.332
and if you're not given the freedom to

00:08:54.332 --> 00:08:58.682
express yourself creatively
and to be vulnerable

00:08:58.810 --> 00:09:01.672
then you kind of hold back
and play it safe sometimes.

00:09:01.672 --> 00:09:05.353
We were given the
opportunity not to be safe.

00:09:07.132 --> 00:09:10.592
♪ Tell me something boy, ♪

00:09:10.592 --> 00:09:15.592
♪ Aren't you tired trying
to fill that void? ♪

00:09:17.332 --> 00:09:20.665
♪ Or do you need more? ♪

